1
00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:03.300
In this lesson, you will

2
00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:06.300
learn about the internals
of the embedding models.

3
00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:09.133
You will see how your texts are converted

4
00:00:09.133 --> 00:00:12.700
into vectors through multiple layers
of an embedding model.

5
00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:15.000
Let's have some fun.

6
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.133
Vector search applications like Retrieval

7
00:00:17.133 --> 00:00:18.300
Augmented Generation,

8
00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.600
hinge on embedding models.

9
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:22.933
Choosing a suitable
one is a pivotal decision

10
00:00:22.933 --> 00:00:24.700
when implementing such projects.

11
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:27.500
If you select the model
trained on academic papers,

12
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:29.800
but all your data
will be coming from Twitter,

13
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:33.100
there is a high chance
that the search quality will be impacted.

14
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.900
Surprisingly, not that many people perform
the evaluations,

15
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.900
which should be one of the first steps.

16
00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:41.633
We will consider
the optimizations of vector search

17
00:00:41.633 --> 00:00:45.600
that everything starts with choosing
the proper embeddings for your data.

18
00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:50.900
The embedding model should take any text
and convert it into a single vector

19
00:00:51.133 --> 00:00:54.500
of fixed dimensionality,
so we can compare the distance

20
00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:57.933
between any two documents
to determine their similarity.

21
00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:01.500
However, embedding
models do not operate on text directly.

22
00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:06.000
Instead, they require
an additional process that splits them

23
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.400
into smaller pieces.

24
00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:10.400
This translation
layer is called a tokenizer.

25
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:11.800
We will have a closer

26
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:16.300
look at the role of the tokenizers
in the whole process of creating the text

27
00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:17.300
embeddings.

28
00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:20.400
This image shows a standard
transformer architecture

29
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:23.400
and comes from
the attention is all you need paper.

30
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.733
It presents a full encoder-decoder model.

31
00:01:27.133 --> 00:01:31.200
However, embedding models
are usually encoder-only transformers.

32
00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:34.533
Let's see how the input is transformed

33
00:01:34.533 --> 00:01:38.033
step by step. These models take an input

34
00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:41.233
and convert it into input embeddings.

35
00:01:41.233 --> 00:01:44.233
These embeddings are learned
during the training process.

36
00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:47.400
Each possible
value of the input has its own embedding.

37
00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:50.700
That means transformers are able to digest

38
00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:53.700
only the input symbols
they were trained on.

39
00:01:53.733 --> 00:01:57.233
In case of the embedding models,
they can only work

40
00:01:57.233 --> 00:02:00.700
with the text units
they encountered in the training data.

41
00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:03.500
If your model was solely trained
on English,

42
00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:06.900
it won't be able to work with Japanese
texts and vice versa.

43
00:02:07.033 --> 00:02:11.633
There are, however, no strict requirements
on how this text units should look like.

44
00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:15.100
There are usually on a word
or subword level.

45
00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:20.100
The inputs are integer identifiers,
so they can be mapped to a certain input

46
00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:25.400
embedding by the model tokenizer expected
to produce a sequence of numerical

47
00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:29.433
IDs corresponding to the tokens
seen in a given text.

48
00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:33.400
This IDs defines some sort of a contract
between the tokenizer

49
00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:35.933
and then voting model itself.

50
00:02:35.933 --> 00:02:38.933
Thus,
we cannot swap the components easily.

51
00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:42.500
Once the training is finished,
as ID matter.

52
00:02:43.033 --> 00:02:45.600
The most straightforward idea to handle
the inputs

53
00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:49.433
would be to treat each individual letter
as a separate entity.

54
00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:54.000
Theoretically, an embedding model
should be able to work on letters or bytes

55
00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:57.600
that would result in a very small number
of tokens.

56
00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:01.900
The size of the vocabulary will also
impact the number of input embeddings

57
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:03.400
learned by the model.

58
00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:04.900
However, each character

59
00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:08.900
may occur in various contexts,
so the embedding won't be meaningful.

60
00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:12.900
The network parameters will need to learn
the relationship

61
00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:16.900
between the letters and derive
what the sequences mean.

62
00:03:17.533 --> 00:03:20.000
In practice,
we want more meaningful pieces,

63
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:23.800
so the transformer inputs are initialized
with some meaning already.

64
00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:28.400
Contrary to character-based tokenization,
we could use words tokens.

65
00:03:28.633 --> 00:03:31.600
Each word would get its own identifier

66
00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:35.400
and the model would have to learn
more embeddings to cover them all.

67
00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:39.233
That means more training data and time
spent on training.

68
00:03:39.333 --> 00:03:42.600
Moreover, we would not be able
to represent any unseen word

69
00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:46.400
as a sequence of tokens
as we would only cover full words

70
00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:50.400
that would never happen with the character
or byte-level tokenization.

71
00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:55.600
This idea ignores the fact that language
has a structure, and certain words

72
00:03:55.933 --> 00:03:59.300
may have some common parts
carrying the meaning.

73
00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:03.700
Subword level tokenization
seems as a compromise between these two

74
00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:05.000
basic approaches.

75
00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:09.300
There are some existing NLP methods,
such as stemming or lemmatization,

76
00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:13.700
that incorporate linguistic knowledge
to find the root forms of the words.

77
00:04:13.833 --> 00:04:17.500
That may sound appealing,
but they won't work for any other language

78
00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:20.300
as they differ in terms of syntax.

79
00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:24.200
However, various algorithms
try to find a way to split the words

80
00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:27.133
based on the statistics
of the training data,

81
00:04:27.133 --> 00:04:30.300
and they are commonly used in embedding
models and LLM's.

82
00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:33.600
We will review the alternatives
in the second lesson,

83
00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:38.300
but ideally, the tokenization algorithm
should find the root forms of the words

84
00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:41.800
and assign them separate tokens.

85
00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:44.633
Let's track the process of converting text

86
00:04:44.633 --> 00:04:47.700
into the input embeddings step by step.

87
00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:52.233
First of all, we split into tokens
using the selected tokenizer.

88
00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:57.500
Then, the tokenizer produces a sequence of
token IDs and sends them to the model.

89
00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:02.700
Model maps each of the token IDs
into the learned input embedding.

90
00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:06.733
The first step of the transformer
is actually a lookup table.

91
00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:10.033
It is a pretty interesting exercise
to see how this input

92
00:05:10.033 --> 00:05:13.300
embeddings will look like for different
input texts.

93
00:05:13.433 --> 00:05:17.000
Let's check one of the pre-trained
sentence transformers.

94
00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:20.200
You will use one of the existing
pre-trained models.

95
00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:22.033
It isn't the benchmark-winning one,

96
00:05:22.033 --> 00:05:25.633
but its open-source nature helps
to deconstruct it into pieces.

97
00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:30.300
Sentence Transformers is a Python library
providing many embedding models,

98
00:05:30.733 --> 00:05:35.433
and you will use all
all-MiniLM-L6-v2 in the whole course.

99
00:05:35.633 --> 00:05:40.633
However, changing it to a different one
is as simple as passing a different name.

100
00:05:41.633 --> 00:05:44.033
This model consists of a transformer,

101
00:05:44.033 --> 00:05:47.200
a meaning pooling
layer, and normalization.

102
00:05:47.433 --> 00:05:51.333
This output hides the tokenizer component,
which is also important.

103
00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:56.400
You can tokenize the text
using a tokenized method of the model.

104
00:05:57.100 --> 00:06:01.900
It accepts multiple text at a time
and requires passing a list of strings.

105
00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:05.900
A list of input IDs

106
00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:08.700
it's not the most straightforward
or human friendly

107
00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:11.900
way of inspecting
how the input text was cut into pieces.

108
00:06:11.933 --> 00:06:15.733
You can see a list of tokens
by passing a list

109
00:06:15.733 --> 00:06:19.900
of corresponding IDs
to the convert IDs to tokens

110
00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:22.900
method of the model's tokenizer.

111
00:06:24.733 --> 00:06:28.033
Each word of our example
has a corresponding token

112
00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:30.633
learned by the tokenizer.

113
00:06:30.633 --> 00:06:34.433
There are also some technical tokens
marked with square brackets.

114
00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:38.233
CLS and SEP start and end each sequence.

115
00:06:38.633 --> 00:06:42.200
Let's forget about the pooling and
normalization layers for the time being.

116
00:06:42.233 --> 00:06:45.700
Accessing the first module helps
to investigate the inputs

117
00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:48.700
a little bit more.

118
00:06:49.633 --> 00:06:50.400
This transformer

119
00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:53.400
model has an embedding layer
at the very beginning.

120
00:06:53.633 --> 00:06:58.033
It is responsible for transforming the raw
input tokens into the embeddings.

121
00:06:58.633 --> 00:07:02.333
This embeddings are then transformed
by multiple stacked layers.

122
00:07:03.333 --> 00:07:03.700
But for

123
00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:07.100
now you will mainly focus on the input
token embeddings.

124
00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:09.000
Let's access them.

125
00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:12.733
The input layer has different
kinds of embeddings, including word

126
00:07:12.733 --> 00:07:16.933
embeddings, position
embeddings, and token-type embeddings.

127
00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:20.800
The word
embeddings are the most important ones

128
00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:23.800
as they capture the meaning of each token.

129
00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:27.000
You can access them
by coding the word embeddings method

130
00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:30.600
on the embeddings layer.

131
00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:32.600
The input token

132
00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:35.600
embeddings for both texts
have been calculated.

133
00:07:35.833 --> 00:07:39.700
These embeddings are calculated
separately for each token in a sequence,

134
00:07:40.133 --> 00:07:43.233
and we can calculate the dissimilarity
between the tokens

135
00:07:43.233 --> 00:07:46.233
based on them.

136
00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:54.000
You can see the input token

137
00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:59.000
embedding does not change, no matter
the context or order of the words.

138
00:08:00.233 --> 00:08:03.600
You can experiment
with passing different text,

139
00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:08.333
different sentences to see how similar
the input token embeddings are.

140
00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:11.533
However, the same token

141
00:08:11.533 --> 00:08:14.400
will always get the same vector.

142
00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:18.100
Now let's recap the whole process done
in an embedding model.

143
00:08:18.433 --> 00:08:20.800
You have just reviewed
the input token embeddings.

144
00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:24.633
But we mostly care about the output
embeddings as they are supposed to

145
00:08:24.633 --> 00:08:26.100
capture the meaning of text.

146
00:08:27.233 --> 00:08:29.400
The sequence of input embeddings

147
00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:32.433
do not contain any positional information.

148
00:08:33.033 --> 00:08:35.000
We want to enrich the token embeddings

149
00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:38.133
so the model can also capture
the positional information.

150
00:08:38.833 --> 00:08:41.833
They might be also some additional
embeddings depending on the model.

151
00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:45.100
This is done
by adding the positional encodings.

152
00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:50.400
As a result, we get a slightly modified
set of token embeddings, which

153
00:08:50.433 --> 00:08:54.533
should also capture the relationships
between the tokens in the whole text.

154
00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:58.800
Positional encodings are usually generated
with a sine function.

155
00:08:59.900 --> 00:09:02.433
Once both input token embeddings

156
00:09:02.433 --> 00:09:05.433
and positional encoding are added,

157
00:09:05.633 --> 00:09:10.133
we pass them through a set of stacked
layers, each of them taken by the input

158
00:09:10.133 --> 00:09:14.500
embeddings and producing some output
embeddings. Internally,

159
00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:17.533
each of these modules uses
an attention mechanism

160
00:09:17.533 --> 00:09:20.533
to determine the relationships
between input embeddings,

161
00:09:20.833 --> 00:09:24.300
so here is where all the cross
token information is captured.

162
00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:29.333
Please note that each model can only take
a certain number of tokens simultaneously.

163
00:09:29.733 --> 00:09:32.800
That's also related
to the positional encodings as they are

164
00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:35.800
kept in a matrix of fixed dimensionality.

165
00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:40.800
If your model was trained to support,
let's say, a maximum of 256 tokens,

166
00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:45.200
then there are no guarantees
it will work for longer sequences.

167
00:09:45.733 --> 00:09:49.300
Usually the tokenizer
will drop the trailing tokens

168
00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:51.800
if the maximum length is exceeded.

169
00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:55.900
Let's visualize the input token embeddings
to get some intuition

170
00:09:55.900 --> 00:09:59.333
on how this mechanism captures
the meaning of tokens.

171
00:10:00.033 --> 00:10:02.600
Input token embeddings are context-free,

172
00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:05.700
and they are also the parameters
of the transformer model.

173
00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:09.000
The model learns them
during the training phase.

174
00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:13.033
To represent the meaning of each token
in the best way possible.

175
00:10:13.500 --> 00:10:15.400
We can access them as a matrix.

176
00:10:21.300 --> 00:10:23.900
Since
the token embeddings are context-free,

177
00:10:23.900 --> 00:10:27.433
we can map each of the vectors
with the corresponding token.

178
00:10:27.933 --> 00:10:32.233
Our matrix has 30,522 inputs.

179
00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:35.600
What is equal
to the size of the vocabulary.

180
00:10:35.900 --> 00:10:38.900
You will now get it from the tokenizer
and then sort

181
00:10:38.900 --> 00:10:41.900
by this index.

182
00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:48.900
This is how some randomly selected

183
00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:51.900
tokens of our model would look like.

184
00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:55.600
The mapping between tokens
and the vectors is now easy.

185
00:10:56.000 --> 00:10:58.633
Visualization is a bit harder
as each vector

186
00:10:58.633 --> 00:11:01.500
has 384 dimensions.

187
00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:04.500
This is a dimensionality
reduction algorithm

188
00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:07.800
you will apply now to compress each vector
to two

189
00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:10.800
dimensional space.

190
00:11:10.933 --> 00:11:12.600
This may take some time.

191
00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:14.700
We have the results.

192
00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:17.800
There are three main groups of tokens
in our vocabulary.

193
00:11:18.233 --> 00:11:20.800
The first group consists
of the technical tokens

194
00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:23.800
specific to the model,
and its training procedure.

195
00:11:24.500 --> 00:11:27.800
Then the subword tokens
with the double hash prefix,

196
00:11:28.333 --> 00:11:32.533
are the suffix tokens particular
to the tokenizer used in our model

197
00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:36.100
Finally, the last group is prefixes

198
00:11:36.100 --> 00:11:39.900
and whole words starting with anything
except the double hash.

199
00:11:40.600 --> 00:11:44.233
You will now assign different colors
depending on the group

200
00:11:44.633 --> 00:11:46.500
a particular token belongs to.

201
00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:49.500
Scatterplot,

202
00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:53.100
using plotly, helps
you interactively visualize them.

203
00:11:53.633 --> 00:11:57.200
When we visualize the inputs
token embeddings we can use it to inspect

204
00:11:57.200 --> 00:11:58.200
the input.

205
00:11:58.200 --> 00:12:00.800
Eyeballing is the only technique
we'll apply here.

206
00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:04.400
But when you check the clusters
you can clearly see that numbers

207
00:12:04.833 --> 00:12:07.400
are put close together.

208
00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:10.700
However, years
form a subgroup of the numbers.

209
00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:14.800
The Cromwell token is closest
to the tokens

210
00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:17.800
representing years from the 16th century.

211
00:12:18.200 --> 00:12:21.400
If you wonder why,
try to google for this English politician.

212
00:12:22.400 --> 00:12:26.500
Subword tokens
occupy some subareas of this space,

213
00:12:27.533 --> 00:12:31.000
but some are just closer to full words.

214
00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:35.500
And used tokens are mixed
with some non-English characters.

215
00:12:37.633 --> 00:12:40.633
Generally, non-English
characters are grouped together.

216
00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:44.700
The names are close to each other.

217
00:12:45.100 --> 00:12:48.500
And actually,
each subarea of the semantic space

218
00:12:49.100 --> 00:12:53.400
holds
tokens with similar meaning or function.

219
00:12:57.333 --> 00:13:00.300
This is
where our embedding model will start.

220
00:13:00.300 --> 00:13:02.900
You can inspect this semantic space
even more

221
00:13:02.900 --> 00:13:05.433
if you want to get some more intuition.

222
00:13:05.433 --> 00:13:09.033
Let's now discuss
all the other steps involved in converting

223
00:13:09.033 --> 00:13:13.600
a sequence of tokens to a vector,
capturing the meaning of the whole text.

224
00:13:14.133 --> 00:13:17.333
We already know that the encoder
only embedding model

225
00:13:17.333 --> 00:13:20.333
starts
with context-independent embeddings.

226
00:13:20.500 --> 00:13:24.200
Attention mechanism captures
the relationships between tokens

227
00:13:24.200 --> 00:13:24.933
and incorporates

228
00:13:24.933 --> 00:13:28.733
that information into the output
embeddings of each stacked module.

229
00:13:29.500 --> 00:13:33.300
Thanks to that, our initial context
independent embeddings

230
00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:38.133
become context-aware, and the embedding
model can produce a sequence of embeddings

231
00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:41.633
with a length equal
to the length of the input sequence.

232
00:13:42.533 --> 00:13:46.133
Pooling helps to create a single vector
of embeddings for a whole text.

233
00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:51.500
It usually averages all the individual
vectors assigned to the input tokens.

234
00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:53.333
Let's verify

235
00:13:53.333 --> 00:13:56.433
whether the output token
embeddings are really context-aware.

236
00:13:57.300 --> 00:14:00.000
When we use the encode
method of the model,

237
00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:03.000
it produces just a single vector.

238
00:14:04.233 --> 00:14:06.333
However, we can also generate a vector

239
00:14:06.333 --> 00:14:10.200
for each individual token,
and those should be context-dependent

240
00:14:10.200 --> 00:14:12.633
already.

241
00:14:12.633 --> 00:14:15.333
We can achieve that
by setting output value

242
00:14:15.333 --> 00:14:18.333
to token embeddings.

243
00:14:19.233 --> 00:14:20.633
If we now check

244
00:14:20.633 --> 00:14:24.200
the output token
embeddings of different sequences,

245
00:14:24.633 --> 00:14:27.633
they should not be identical anymore.

246
00:14:28.033 --> 00:14:31.733
Our embedding model captured
the meaning of each tokens in a sequence,

247
00:14:32.300 --> 00:14:35.400
so they will be encoded differently
depending on the context.

248
00:14:35.900 --> 00:14:39.200
It's not a basic word
to vet like mapping anymore.

249
00:14:39.800 --> 00:14:42.800
We will use the same examples like before,

250
00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:47.000
but you can play and experiment
with different examples if you want.

251
00:14:47.600 --> 00:14:50.900
Then we will tokenize both sentences

252
00:14:51.233 --> 00:14:54.233
using the tokenized method of our model,

253
00:14:54.433 --> 00:14:59.133
and finally, calculate
the embeddings of both sentences.

254
00:14:59.500 --> 00:15:02.333
We are going to specifically retrieve

255
00:15:02.333 --> 00:15:06.300
the token embeddings
produced for each token in both sequences.

256
00:15:06.633 --> 00:15:09.733
Finally, cosine similarity calculated

257
00:15:10.200 --> 00:15:12.800
between each tokens

258
00:15:12.800 --> 00:15:17.200
coming from the first sentence
and each token from the second one.

259
00:15:18.300 --> 00:15:19.000
These distances

260
00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:23.733
will help us to visualize how similar
are different tokens

261
00:15:23.900 --> 00:15:26.900
in both sequences.

262
00:15:29.100 --> 00:15:32.800
The output token embeddings of vector
search

263
00:15:32.800 --> 00:15:36.033
and optimization are not identical
anymore.

264
00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:40.600
You can test different sentences
and see how the output token

265
00:15:40.600 --> 00:15:43.600
embeddings
will vary in terms of their similarity.

266
00:15:44.800 --> 00:15:48.433
Tokenization is an important yet
often ignored step.

267
00:15:49.300 --> 00:15:53.100
It defines how our model sees the world
and how much meaning

268
00:15:53.100 --> 00:15:56.500
each individual
token can initially get in the model.

269
00:15:56.900 --> 00:16:01.033
You will learn about different ways
of tokenization in the next lesson.

270
00:16:02.200 --> 00:16:03.133
All right. See you there.

